4-Week Beginner Plan: Learn GitHub using your Digital_Library project

Overview
- Use the existing Digital_Library project as the reference.
- Copy it into a new repository called Digital_Library_GitHub.
- Each tiny task maps to real files and actions in the new repo so you learn while you work on your app.

Week 1 — Copy and explore the project
- Day 1: Import Digital_Library into Digital_Library_GitHub. Result: you have a copy of the project on GitHub.
- Day 2: Open the Code view and read the README (or create one if missing). Write one sentence describing the project in the README and commit.
- Day 3: Find one small file (example: a sample data file or hello.txt). Edit it in the web UI and commit.
- Day 4: Create an Issue titled "Check project files" listing three files to review (README, .github/workflows, one code file).
- Day 5: Learn about branches — create a branch called explore-files and switch to it in the web UI.
- Day 6–7: Practice editing a file on the branch and open a Pull Request (PR) to merge to default branch. Merge the PR.

Week 2 — Add simple documentation and templates
- Day 1: Add or improve README.md to explain what the Digital Library app does (copy/paste template available).
- Day 2: Add an ISSUE_TEMPLATE or templates/issue_update_metadata.md and create one issue using it.
- Day 3: Create a folder docs/ and add one short doc about running or viewing the app (README or quick-start).
- Day 4: Create labels you will use: enhancement, bug, docs, help wanted.
- Day 5–7: Make 1–2 issues for small enhancements or bugs and practice assigning and closing them.

Week 3 — Work with code and CI workflow files
- Day 1: Open the project's workflow files (.github/workflows/) and read what they do (or add a note in the README describing them).
- Day 2: Create a tiny change in code (small comment or text change) on a new branch and open a PR.
- Day 3: Review PR and merge it. See how merge adds the change to the default branch.
- Day 4: If the project has tests or checks, view the PR checks and read the status. Do not worry about failures yet — create an issue “Fix CI if needed”.
- Day 5–7: Tidy one file: fix spelling in a README or metadata and commit via a branch + PR.

Week 4 — Publish, plan enhancement, and next steps
- Day 1: Add a simple index.html to the repo root or docs/ for a web preview (if you want to publish a minimal site).
- Day 2: Enable GitHub Pages (if you want a simple page for the library). Publish and check the URL.
- Day 3: Create a short project roadmap issue: "4 small enhancements to rebuild the app" — list 4 tiny tasks you can do one per week.
- Day 4–7: Pick one item from the roadmap, create a branch, work on it, open a PR, and merge.

Notes
- When editing, prefer the web UI on your phone browser (Request desktop site if necessary).
- If you want commit history preserved, use the Import option. If not, copy files manually.
- If anything is confusing, say "explain like I'm 5" and I will simplify.
